      "response": "To find the positive value of \\( b \\) such that the equation \\(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\\) has exactly one solution, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Rewrite the equation in a more manageable form:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\n   \\]\n   To eliminate the fractions, multiply both sides by \\(3x\\):\n   \\[\n   3x \\left( \\frac{3}{x} \\right) + 3x \\left( \\frac{x}{3} \\right) = 3x \\cdot b\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   9 + x^2 = 3bx\n   \\]\n   Rearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the conditions for the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution:**\n   A quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) has exactly one solution if and only if its discriminant is zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -3b\\), and \\(c = 9\\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = (-3b)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 9\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 9b^2 - 36\n   \\]\n   Set the discriminant equal to zero for the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution:\n   \\[\n   9b^2 - 36 = 0\n   \\]\n   Solve for \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   9b^2 = 36\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\pm 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Select the positive value of \\(b\\):**\n   Since we are asked for the positive value of \\(b\\), we choose:\n   \\[\n   b = 2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\(b\\) such that the equation \\(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\\) has exactly one solution is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
